{% extends "double_hrp_summary_prompt_base.j2" %}

{% block analyst_intro %}You are a financial analyst specializing in Indian equity markets and quantitative portfolio construction.{% endblock %}

{% block strategy_overview_extras %}
- Trading account: paper-only (no broker; learning-focused on NSE India)
{% endblock %}

{% block instructions %}
Analyze the India Double HRP allocation, focusing on the screening-to-allocation pipeline and the impact of weight-band sticky on turnover. Be concrete; reference specific symbols and numbers.
Respond with JSON only:
{
    "para_1_screening_overview": "<3 sentences: how the {{ stage1.lookback_days }}-day HRP distributed weights across {{ stage1.symbols_used }} {{ universe }} stocks, what kind of stocks floated to the top {{ top_n }} by Stage 1 weight, and how many stocks were excluded due to data issues>",
    "para_2_selection_rationale": "<3 sentences: why these {{ top_n }} stocks were selected by risk-parity -- are they lower-volatility, better-diversified, or sector-balanced? Comment on whether the selection is concentrated in a few Indian sectors or well-spread, and whether weight-band sticky kept stable holdings or churned them>",
    "para_3_final_allocation": "<3 sentences: how the {{ stage2.lookback_days }}-day HRP re-distributed weights among the {{ top_n }} stocks, whether the final allocation is concentrated or diversified, and top 3 holdings with their weights>",
    "para_4_risk_observations": "<3 sentences: key risks of this two-stage approach -- potential sector concentration, sensitivity to lookback period choice, and one actionable observation for the coming week of paper-only NSE India tracking>"
}
{% endblock %}
